# REPRO: a declared `set_ndr` SCOPE that governs no net, and says so.
#
# `docs/internal/opens_ndr.md` §2's residual — "a SCOPE that matches no net is
# a different silence and is still unreported" — plus the second shape found
# while building it, in one run.
#
# ── The condition ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `set_ndr` takes a net-name PREFIX.  A typo attaches a perfectly good rule to
# nothing at all: the rule is declared, accepted, persisted and reported by
# `dump_ndr`, and the design routes ungoverned while every command says
# success.  Nothing looked at scopes from the other end — the governed-bundle
# count says how many bundles a rule reached, never that a SCOPE reached zero.
#
# ── The shapes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   scope      matches   governs   verdict
#   ---------  --------  --------  ------------------------------------------
#   algn       0 nets    0         BUDA-1915 (WARNING) — the typo (`align`)
#   bus_       8 nets    0         BUDA-1916 (INFO) — shadowed by bus_hi_/bus_lo_
#   bus_hi     4 nets    4         silent — it governs
#   bus_lo     4 nets    4         silent — it governs
#   align      4 nets    4         silent — the control
#   *          all       0         silent — EXEMPT, see below
#
# `align` is the control and the reason the run is evidence about the scopes
# rather than about the vehicle: it is the same declaration form as `algn`,
# one character apart, and it says nothing.
#
# `bus_` is the shape a rule-level check cannot see.  Every scope here names
# the SAME rule `em`, and `em` governs 12 bundles — so "is the rule used?"
# answers yes while the `bus_` declaration does nothing whatsoever.  Which
# DECLARATION did the work is a different question from what governs a net,
# which is why the report resolves the winning PREFIX and not just the rule.
#
# `*` is the documented false alarm, and it is why this needed thinking about
# before it needed coding.  `*` is the global default, outranked by any real
# prefix BY CONSTRUCTION, so a design whose every net matches a longer prefix
# leaves it legitimately unused.  Warning about that fires on correct designs
# — and a diagnostic that cries on a correct design is one a methodology
# learns to filter, taking the case it exists for with it.  Here `*` governs
# nothing and is silent.
#
# ── What to do about it ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A REPORT, not a refusal: whether a scope bites is a property of the netlist,
# so refusing would reject designs that route today.  1915 is a WARNING (a
# scope naming nothing cannot be deliberate); 1916 is an INFO (a layered set
# of scopes can shadow one on purpose), and it names an example shadowing
# prefix, since once you know a scope is outranked the only question left is
# by what.

add_block a1 0    0    200  200
add_block b1 0    600  200  800
add_block a2 600  0    800  200
add_block b2 600  600  800  800
add_block a3 1200 0    1400 200
add_block b3 1200 600  1400 800

# Vertically aligned pairs, so each bus is a straight V run.
add_bus align[4]  a1.p b1.q
add_bus bus_hi[4] a2.p b2.q
add_bus bus_lo[4] a3.p b3.q

def_layer 3 M3 H 20
def_layer 4 M4 V 20
def_layer 5 M5 H TOP 20
def_layer 6 M6 V TOP 20

def_track_pattern 3 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1
def_track_pattern 4 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1
def_track_pattern 5 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1
def_track_pattern 6 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1

# One rule for every scope, on purpose: it is what makes the `bus_` case
# unreachable from the rule's side.  A MULTIPLIER rule, so it is active on
# every layer and no BUDA-1913/1914 verdict shares the output.
def_ndr em width x2

set_ndr align   em
set_ndr algn    em
set_ndr bus_    em
set_ndr bus_hi  em
set_ndr bus_lo  em
set_ndr *       em

# The verdicts print HERE — at bundling, before a planner pass is spent.
run_bundler STRICT
dump_ndr

generate_topologies
set_track_pitch 3
run_planner 1
run_nuts
run_detailed_nuts

check_design
report_wirelength
